Deliver the architected timer through a GICv2, so timed sleeps can wake.

XNU's arm64 code never asks an interrupt controller for the timer. It programs
the architected timer itself and assumes the signal comes back as an FIQ:

    osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
      _enable_virtual_timer()   msr CNTP_CTL_EL0, CNTP_CTL_EL0_ENABLE
      ml_set_decrementer()      msr CNTP_TVAL_EL0, dec_value
                                (cpu_set_decrementer_func is never assigned
                                 anywhere in the tree, so the mimetic path is
                                 always the one taken)

    osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
      sleh_fiq()                ml_get_timer_pending() -> rtclock_intr(TRUE)

That works because Apple's AIC routes the timer straight to FIQ. A stock ARM
SoC does not: the EL1 non-secure physical timer is PPI 30 on a GIC, and from
non-secure EL1 an interrupt cannot be placed in Group 0, so it can only ever
be signalled as an IRQ. The IRQ path is

    fleh_irq -> sleh_irq -> cpu_data_t::interrupt_handler

and that handler is installed by IOCPUInterruptController::enableCPUInterrupt,
from a platform kext this kernel does not have. The pexpert side offers no way
out either: pe_arm_init_timer() only fills in rtclock_timebase_func, which on
arm64 is dead weight -- fleh_fiq_generic is literally PANIC_UNIMPLEMENTED in
osfmk/arm64/locore.s, and the tbd_ops hooks are an arm32 mechanism.

So on a GIC-based SoC the kernel comes up with a running countdown that can
never be delivered. Nothing is visibly wrong until the first code that sleeps
on a deadline rather than on a wakeup:

    bsd_init() -> IOKitInitializeTime()
      t.tv_sec = 30;
      IOService::waitForService(IOService::resourceMatching("IORTC"), &t);
        -> waitForMatchingService(matching, 30 * NSEC_PER_SEC)
        -> SLEEPNOTIFYTO(&result, deadline)
        -> assert_wait_deadline() + thread_block()

There is no IORTC and there never will be, so the whole 30 seconds has to be
counted out by the timer interrupt. Without one the sleep is permanent, and
the boot stops at "bsd_init: calling IOKitInitializeTime" with no panic, no
reboot and no further output. Every earlier wait in the boot was woken by
another thread, which is why nothing failed before this line.

This patch does the minimum to close that gap on the boot CPU:

  - masks and clears every interrupt the bootloader left behind, so opening
    the CPU interface does not immediately storm a handler that knows nothing
    about them;
  - unmasks PPI 30 and nothing else, at the highest priority non-secure EL1
    is permitted to request;
  - enables the distributor and the CPU interface in their non-secure view;
  - installs an IRQ handler that acknowledges the GIC, hands a timer
    interrupt to rtclock_intr() exactly as sleh_fiq() would, and EOIs.

The handler is written into BootCpuData directly rather than through
ml_install_interrupt_handler(), because that function ends with
initialize_screen(NULL, kPEAcquireScreen) and would tear down the framebuffer
console at a point in the boot where it is the only output there is.

It runs from arm_init(), immediately after fiq_context_bootstrap(TRUE) has
enabled the timer and while interrupts are still masked, and immediately
after PE_init_platform(TRUE, ...) so that ml_io_map() is usable -- that is the
same point at which pe_arm_map_interrupt_controller() maps its own registers.

The register addresses are this board's and are the one thing to change when
porting: GICD 0x104E1000 and GICC 0x104E2000, read out of the stock Android
device tree for the Exynos 7570 (the same addresses the Exynos 7870 upstream
DT carries). Everything else is architectural GICv2.

Boot CPU only. SGIs are not configured and the banked PPI registers are
programmed once, which is enough for cpus=1; bringing up secondary CPUs will
need the per-CPU half repeated from arm_init_cpu().

--- a/osfmk/arm/arm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/arm_init.c
@@ -270,6 +270,158 @@
 	j3_band(J3_MARK2_ROW0, slot, colour);
 }
 
+/*
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Minimal GICv2 bring-up for the boot CPU's timer interrupt.
+ *
+ * XNU's arm64 code expects the architected timer to reach the core by itself:
+ * fiq_context_init() sets CNTP_CTL_EL0.ENABLE, ml_set_decrementer() writes
+ * CNTP_TVAL_EL0 directly (cpu_set_decrementer_func is never assigned on
+ * arm64), and the SoC is expected to deliver the resulting signal as an FIQ,
+ * where sleh_fiq() hands it to rtclock_intr(). That is how Apple's AIC is
+ * wired.
+ *
+ * A stock ARM SoC routes the same timer through a GIC as PPI 30, the
+ * non-secure EL1 physical timer. From non-secure EL1 an interrupt cannot be
+ * moved into Group 0, so it can only ever arrive as an IRQ:
+ * fleh_irq -> sleh_irq -> cpu_data's interrupt_handler.
+ *
+ * With no GIC set up, nothing looks wrong until the first code that sleeps on
+ * a deadline. IOKitInitializeTime()'s 30-second waitForService(IORTC) never
+ * times out, and the boot stops there with no panic and no further output.
+ *
+ * So: mask everything the bootloader left enabled, unmask PPI 30 and nothing
+ * else, open the distributor and the CPU interface, and install a handler
+ * that acknowledges the GIC and calls rtclock_intr() the way sleh_fiq()
+ * would have.
+ *
+ * Boot CPU only. SGIs are not configured and the banked PPI registers are
+ * only programmed here, which is enough for cpus=1; secondary CPUs will need
+ * the same done from arm_init_cpu().
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+#define GIC_DIST_PA             0x104E1000UL    /* Exynos 7570 GICD, from the stock DT */
+#define GIC_CPU_PA              0x104E2000UL    /* Exynos 7570 GICC */
+#define GIC_MMIO_SIZE           0x1000UL
+
+#define GICD_CTLR               0x000
+#define GICD_TYPER              0x004
+#define GICD_ISENABLER          0x100
+#define GICD_ICENABLER          0x180
+#define GICD_ICPENDR            0x280
+#define GICD_IPRIORITYR         0x400
+
+#define GICC_CTLR               0x000
+#define GICC_PMR                0x004
+#define GICC_BPR                0x008
+#define GICC_IAR                0x00C
+#define GICC_EOIR               0x010
+
+#define GIC_TIMER_PPI           30              /* non-secure EL1 physical timer */
+#define GIC_INTID_MASK          0x3FF
+#define GIC_SPURIOUS            0x3FF
+
+static vm_offset_t      j3_gicd;
+static vm_offset_t      j3_gicc;
+
+/* Inspectable from a panic report; proves interrupts are arriving at all. */
+volatile uint64_t       j3_gic_irqs;
+
+static inline uint32_t
+j3_gic_read(vm_offset_t base, uint32_t off)
+{
+	return *(volatile uint32_t *)(base + off);
+}
+
+static inline void
+j3_gic_write(vm_offset_t base, uint32_t off, uint32_t val)
+{
+	*(volatile uint32_t *)(base + off) = val;
+}
+
+static void
+j3_gic_timer_handler(void *target __unused, void *refCon __unused,
+    void *nub __unused, int source __unused)
+{
+	uint32_t        iar, intid;
+
+	for (;;) {
+		iar = j3_gic_read(j3_gicc, GICC_IAR);
+		intid = iar & GIC_INTID_MASK;
+
+		/* A spurious ID means the queue is empty, and must not be EOI'd. */
+		if (intid == GIC_SPURIOUS) {
+			break;
+		}
+
+		j3_gic_irqs++;
+
+		if (intid == GIC_TIMER_PPI) {
+			/*
+			 * Mirror sleh_fiq(): drop the software shadow of the
+			 * decrementer before rtclock_intr() reprograms it.
+			 */
+			getCpuDatap()->cpu_decrementer = -1;
+			rtclock_intr(TRUE);
+		}
+
+		j3_gic_write(j3_gicc, GICC_EOIR, iar);
+	}
+}
+
+static void
+j3_gic_init(void)
+{
+	uint32_t        typer, blocks, i;
+
+	j3_gicd = ml_io_map(GIC_DIST_PA, GIC_MMIO_SIZE);
+	j3_gicc = ml_io_map(GIC_CPU_PA, GIC_MMIO_SIZE);
+
+	/* ITLinesNumber counts 32-interrupt blocks, less one. */
+	typer = j3_gic_read(j3_gicd, GICD_TYPER);
+	blocks = (typer & 0x1F) + 1;
+
+	/*
+	 * The bootloader leaves its own interrupts enabled and possibly
+	 * pending. None of them has a driver here, so mask and clear the lot
+	 * before the CPU interface is opened, or the first enable turns into
+	 * an interrupt storm into a handler that knows nothing about them.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < blocks; i++) {
+		j3_gic_write(j3_gicd, GICD_ICENABLER + 4 * i, 0xFFFFFFFF);
+		j3_gic_write(j3_gicd, GICD_ICPENDR + 4 * i, 0xFFFFFFFF);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Priority is written through the non-secure view, where the value is
+	 * shifted right and the top bit forced on, so 0 here is the highest
+	 * priority this exception level is allowed to ask for.
+	 */
+	*(volatile uint8_t *)(j3_gicd + GICD_IPRIORITYR + GIC_TIMER_PPI) = 0x00;
+
+	j3_gic_write(j3_gicd, GICD_ISENABLER, 1U << GIC_TIMER_PPI);
+	j3_gic_write(j3_gicd, GICD_CTLR, 1);            /* non-secure view: EnableGrp1 */
+
+	j3_gic_write(j3_gicc, GICC_PMR, 0xF0);          /* pass every non-secure priority */
+	j3_gic_write(j3_gicc, GICC_BPR, 0x07);          /* no sub-priority preemption */
+	j3_gic_write(j3_gicc, GICC_CTLR, 1);            /* non-secure view: EnableGrp1 */
+
+	/*
+	 * Installed by hand rather than through ml_install_interrupt_handler(),
+	 * which ends in initialize_screen(NULL, kPEAcquireScreen) and would
+	 * take the framebuffer console away this early in the boot.
+	 */
+	BootCpuData.interrupt_handler = j3_gic_timer_handler;
+	BootCpuData.interrupt_nub = NULL;
+	BootCpuData.interrupt_source = GIC_TIMER_PPI;
+	BootCpuData.interrupt_target = NULL;
+	BootCpuData.interrupt_refCon = NULL;
+	BootCpuData.interrupts_enabled = TRUE;
+
+	kprintf("j3_gic_init: GICD %p GICC %p, %u lines, PPI %u enabled\n",
+	    (void *)j3_gicd, (void *)j3_gicc, blocks * 32, GIC_TIMER_PPI);
+}
+
 void
 arm_init(
 	boot_args       *args)
@@ -541,6 +693,8 @@
 	cpu_timebase_init(TRUE);
 	fiq_context_bootstrap(TRUE);
 
+	j3_gic_init();
+
 
 	/*
 	 * Initialize the stack protector for all future calls
